  ],
  "text": "You see a room. A dog smells who was in it, and how long ago they left. You have about six million smell receptors. A dog has three hundred million. And a far bigger part of the brain doing the work on them. It also has a second organ, just for the chemicals other animals leave behind. And it smells separately with each nostril, which gives the smell a direction. Here is the part we have no equivalent for. A scent trail contains time. The oldest molecules have faded. The newest ones are still strong. So a dog does not just find the trail. It reads which way it was walked. For a dog, a room is not a place. It is a recording. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
